  "strategy": "Approach and rationale\nThe central idea is to minimize the AC1 bound for a non-negative step-function by iteratively refining a candidate sequence using a combination of linear programming (LP) and gradient-based optimization. The previous program achieved a bound of 1.522031, while the target is 1.50274349. Our method builds on the reference LP-based optimization but introduces adaptive dimension adjustment and multi-phase updates to reduce stagnation. \n\n- **LP-based updates**: We formulate an optimization problem where we maximize the dual sequence subject to convolution constraints, then combine it with the current sequence.\n- **Gradient descent**: We use gradient information to refine the sequence locally, addressing expensive convolution computations via FFT.\n- **Adaptive dimension**: We allow sequence lengths up to 256 with automatic resizing, handling various grid sizes efficiently.\n- **Fallback mechanisms**: We preserve the parent sequence (96-length) if extens have finite time and resources.\n- **Budget-aware scheduling**: We use time budgeting to balance exploration and exploitation.\n\nMathematical specification\n**Variables**: Coefficients \\( a_0,\\dots,a_{n-1} \\) of a non-negative real vector.\n**Objective**: Minimize \\( 2n \\|a \\star a\\|_{\\infty} / (\\sum a_i)^2 \\).\n**Constraints**: All coefficients are in [0, 1000.0] and \\( \\sum a_i \\geq 0.01 \\).\n**Optimization**: Two-phase method combining LP and gradient descent:\n- **LP phase**: Solve \\( \\max \\sum g_i \\) to improve expected performance.\n- **Gradient phase**: Local refinement using finite differences and line search.\n\nImplementation procedure\n1. Add parent sequence if provided and valid.\n2. Define a budget distribution based on the initial sequence length.\n3. For each candidate length:\n   - Initialize with parent or Dirichlet + normalized clipping.\n   - Solve an LP to update the sequence.\n   - Apply gradient steps if LP solution improves.\n   - Verify candidates with exact evaluation and update best solution.\n4. Compare across lengths, keeping the minimal bound.\n5. Terminate when budget expires or no improvement for",
